/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> setFillDoubleBassLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "In metal the feet take part in the fill: the hands play a beat or two on the drums, then the double bass finishes the bar as a sixteenth roll. It is a huge sound for little hand movement, and it trains the switch between hands and feet that every faster metal fill needs.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Three bars of rock sixteenths, crash on the one of bar 1. In the fill bar line 1 plays R L R L on the snare for beat 1, on the high tom for beat 2, then the feet take beats 3 and 4 with alternating sixteenths, right foot on every beat and left foot in between. Hands and feet never overlap; the right foot starts on the beat right after the last left-hand note.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Line 2 plays one beat of hands and three beats of feet, line 3 three beats of hands and one of feet. Line 4 swaps hands and feet every beat, line 5 starts with the feet and ends with the hands on high tom and floor tom. Line 6 does the same in sextuplets, line 7 puts thirty-seconds in the hands before the feet, and line 8 is a whole bar of feet into the crash. Keep both feet even; if the left foot is weaker, practise line 8 slowly first.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Thrash and death metal: Dave Lombardo with Slayer, Gene Hoglan with Death and Strapping Young Lad, and the hand-and-feet fills of modern metal drummers in almost every breakdown.",
  ),
];
